INT. FENN & SON UPHOLSTERY - REAR LOADING DOCK - NIGHT

Bolts of fabric stacked to the rafters. A single hanging bulb. The roll-up door is half open to the rain.

LAZLO FENN, 50s, a heavy-set man in suspenders, counts bills at a workbench. RAY OBUYA, 30s, in a leather jacket, leans against a stack of velvet, thumbing a lighter on and off.

RAY
It's short.

LAZLO
It isn't.

RAY
Count it again.

Lazlo does not look up. He finishes the stack, squares the edges against the bench, and starts a second.

LAZLO
Your boss has a long memory. So do I.

RAY
My boss said four. That's three-eight.

LAZLO
It's four.

Ray pushes off the velvet and takes one step. He is close enough now to see the sweat on the other man's collar.

RAY
Then why are you counting like a man who got robbed?

LAZLO
Because I'm careful.

RAY
You've been careful for twenty years and I never once saw you count twice.

Lazlo stops. He lays both hands flat on the money. Behind him, a roll of fabric slumps on its stand.

RAY (CONT'D)
Who's behind the roll?

LAZLO
Nobody.

RAY
He's smaller than you. He's breathing too hard.

LAZLO
Ray. Let it go.

Ray draws a revolver from the small of his back and lowers it, slowly, toward the floor.

RAY
Come out of there and I won't ask where the other two hundred went.

The roll shifts. A boy, thin, no more than sixteen, steps out into the light with a fistful of cash held against his chest.
